#: ../../source/weather_SKILL.md:8
msgid "Weather"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/weather_SKILL.md:10
msgid ""
"Use the most reliable location match first. For Chinese city names or "
"other non-Latin input, prefer `wttr.in` with the original query because "
"it resolves native names directly. Use Open-Meteo for structured current "
"conditions and forecasts only after you have confirmed the exact city."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/weather_SKILL.md:12
msgid "Accuracy Rules"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/weather_SKILL.md:14
msgid ""
"Always restate the matched location, region/country, and observation time"
" in the final answer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/weather_SKILL.md:15
msgid ""
"Do not trust the first geocoding hit blindly. Check `country`, `admin1`, "
"`admin2`, and `population`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/weather_SKILL.md:16
msgid ""
"For Chinese city queries, do not send Hanzi directly to Open-Meteo "
"geocoding unless the top result is obviously correct. Prefer `wttr.in` "
"with the original Chinese name, or geocode the English/pinyin city name "
"instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/weather_SKILL.md:17
msgid ""
"If multiple plausible matches remain, ask a follow-up question or state "
"the assumption clearly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/weather_SKILL.md:18
msgid ""
"Use `timezone=auto` when calling Open-Meteo so the reported time matches "
"the location."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/weather_SKILL.md:20
msgid "wttr.in (best for direct city-name queries)"
